-- title:
--   Characteristic-zero primes of the Hecke–diamond ring are eigencharacters
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be an integral domain of characteristic zero, let $M\ge 1$, let $H,H'\le(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$ be subgroups and let $S\subseteq\mathbb N$. Write $V_A=\operatorname{Hom}(\Gamma_H(M),A)$ for the additive characters of $\Gamma_H(M)$, the subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ of elements of $\Gamma_0(M)$ whose lower-right unit modulo $M$ lies in $H$, and let $\mathbb T\subseteq\operatorname{End}_{\mathcal O}(V_{\mathcal O})$ be the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra generated by the operators `heckeTL` at primes $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid M$ together with the diamond operators $\langle u\rangle$ (precomposition with conjugation by a chosen element of $\Gamma_0(M)$ with lower-right unit $u$) for $u\in H'$. Assume these generators commute pairwise. Let $\mathfrak p\subset\mathbb T$ be a prime ideal containing no nonzero integer, and let $\Omega$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero. Then there exist a ring homomorphism $\chi\colon\mathbb T\to\Omega$, a character $e\colon(\mathbb Z/M)^\times\to\Omega^\times$ and a nonzero $\varphi\in V_\Omega$ such that $\ker\chi=\mathfrak p$; for every prime $\ell\notin S$ with $\ell\nmid M$ the operator `heckeT` on $V_\Omega$ sends $\varphi$ to $\chi(T_\ell)\varphi$; $\langle u\rangle\varphi=e(u)\varphi$ for every $u\in(\mathbb Z/M)^\times$; and $e(u)=\chi(\langle u\rangle)$ for $u\in H'$.
--
--   This is the algebraic half of the dictionary between points of the integral Hecke–diamond ring acting on $H^1(\Gamma_H(M),\mathcal O)$ and systems of Hecke eigenvalues: a prime of residue characteristic zero is realised by a simultaneous eigenvector over any algebraically closed field of characteristic zero, and the eigenvector may be taken to be an eigenvector for the full group of diamond operators, in the style of Deligne–Serre. It is used in the passage from Hecke characters to nebentype information and, via the auxiliary- and Taylor–Wiles-level Hecke rings, in the production of eigenforms attached to algebra homomorphisms.
